---
title: Exif reader
tags: tools
---
{% from "macros/tool.njk" import toolSection with context %}
{% from "macros/tool-frame.njk" import fileFrame %}
{% set filename = page.fileSlug %}
{% set intro %}
<p class="px-2">
    The Exif reader tool allows you to pull metadata information from a variety of image file types, such as GPS information, camera information (like exposure, aperture, device make and model), and more. This metadata can often be where puzzle makers hide information.
</p>
<p class="px-2">
    Text fields, such as comments and descriptions, are shown first, and any value that looks encoded (as binary, hex or Base64) is marked, with a link that opens it in <a href="/convrtrs/" class="text-convrtr" data-convrtrs-link>Convrtrs</a> to decode. Every tag follows, grouped by where it's stored in the file.
</p>
<p class="px-2">
    {%- set supported_file_types = "JPEG,TIFF,PNG,HEIC,AVIF,WebP,GIF".split(",") %}
    The Exif reader supports the following file types:
    {%- for fileType in supported_file_types %}
    <code
        class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">{{ fileType }}</code>
    {%- endfor %}
</p>
<blockquote>
    <p class="ms-2">&mdash; Powered by <a href="https://github.com/mattiasw/ExifReader"
            class="text-convrtr">ExifReader</a> by Mattias Wallander, available under the <a
            href="https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/" class="text-convrtr">Mozilla Public License Version 2.0</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
{% endset %}
{% call toolSection(intro) %}
{% call fileFrame(filename, "Exif reader", formats = "JPEG, TIFF, PNG, HEIC, AVIF, WebP or GIF", privacy = "Read in your browser. Your image is never uploaded.", accept = "image/*,.heic,.heif,.avif") %}{% endcall %}
{% endcall %}
